mechanism.text:
Preparing graph-structured inputs that expose both atom- and bond-centric context for a graph transformer and its pretraining tasks, such as GROVER, is the concrete goal: extract_grover_attributes converts a batched molecular graph into the arrays and index mappings the model needs for node/edge features, directed-edge message passing, and motif/functional-group labels.

At a high level the function implements a canonicalization and indexing layer: it flattens a batch of molecule graphs into contiguous feature tensors and integer index maps that support fast lookups for (1) atom → incident bonds, (2) bond → source atom, (3) bond → reverse (opposite) bond, and (4) atom → neighboring atoms. These structures are the low-level data the GROVER architecture uses to run edge-aware message passing and to compute context-sensitive edge/node representations for transformer-style encoders.

Algorithmically, the routine computes:
- atom_features: per-atom numeric vectors (one-hot types, formal charge, aromaticity, etc.);
- bond_features: per-directed-bond vectors (bond type, conjugation, ring flags), typically stored for both directions of each undirected chemical bond;
- atom_to_bond: for each atom index, a list (or padded slice) of directed-bond indices that originate/incident at that atom;
- bond_to_atom: mapping from each directed-bond to its target/source atom index used by message aggregation;
- bond_to_reverse: for each directed bond index, the index of the bond representing the opposite direction (useful for excluding backtracking or for computing pairwise edge features);
- atom_to_atom: neighbor atom indices, sometimes derived from atom_to_bond via bond_to_atom;
- atom/bond scopes: per-molecule start+length ranges so batch elements can be isolated during loss or pooling;
- functional group labels and extra features: precomputed group membership masks used in GROVER’s motif-aware pretraining.

Key design decisions: represent bonds as directed edges to simplify message passing and attention over incoming edges; provide compact integer mappings instead of sparse matrices for memory and GPU efficiency; include reverse-bond links to enable exclusion of immediate backtracking and to compute symmetric pair features cheaply; and supply “scopes” to preserve batched locality while using flat tensors. These choices trade some preprocessing cost for fast, memory-coherent GPU kernels at training/inference time.

Use this utility when feeding molecular BatchGraphData into GROVER-style models or any model requiring directed-edge mappings and motif labels. Alternatives include adjacency/sparse-matrix representations or libraries that keep per-graph Python objects; those are simpler but slower for large batches or GPU-heavy pipelines. Assumptions: input is chemically valid BatchGraphData with consistent atom/bond indexing, and features expected by downstream GROVER layers (directed-bond duplication, consistent reverse-bond pairing, and available functional-group annotations).

